From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:04:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] tests/color-management: Cover gamut compression and scanout
 parity

Extend the color-hdr conformance test. Expected values come from the
ACES 1.3 Reference Gamut Compression written out in the test, in double
precision: RGB to XYZ matrices built from the chromaticities, Bradford
adaptation of the encoding's white point to the renderer's D65 as
pixels are converted, the per-channel limits found by sampling every
side of the source gamut densely (100000 points per side), containment
with per-primary tolerances, and the published scale and compression
curve (threshold 0.8, power 1.2). The target is mostly a P3-D65-like
gamut, declared exactly, whose blue primary lies just beyond sRGB's: it
contains sRGB but not BT.2020. Blending states are built the way the
renderer view builds them, from the output color state with the target
primaries (and optionally the sample target peak) attached:

 - /color-hdr/gamut/predicate: BT.2020, primaries 0.1% beyond a target
   vertex, and BT.2020 on a declared sRGB target are compressed; sRGB,
   content in the target's own primaries and sRGB on a declared sRGB
   target (on the boundary within the declaration's resolution, after
   adaptation to the renderer's D65), BT.2020 content mastered with sRGB
   primaries and sRGB content mastered with BT.2020 primaries are not;
   no compression without target primaries, toward the output state
   (also with the primaries attached directly, without a display
   reference), or from the blending state to the output or to itself;
   clutter_color_transform_requires_mapping() is the union of both
   predicates; the target primaries and their tolerances are part of the
   exact identity; with both a peak and primaries the EETF precedes the
   compression.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/identity: when nothing is compressed, the conversion
   through the transform cache is bit-identical to the one toward the
   blending state without target primaries.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/boundary: BT.2020, whose sides reach further than
   its primaries in the target, is converted along its whole boundary
   (4001 points per side, PQ encoded) to the forced-linear blending
   state: no point ends beyond distance 1, and each channel's maximising
   point lands at 1.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/non-d65: DCI-P3 with the DCI white, whose
   chromaticities are P3's, lands partly outside a P3-D65 target once
   adapted and is compressed; ProPhoto (D50) too; both boundaries land
   on the target as above.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/extended-range: extended-range linear sRGB and
   BT.2020 without mastering primaries are not compressed; extended sRGB
   mastered on BT.2020 is, with the limits of BT.2020 adapted as sRGB's
   white is, and the BT.2020 boundary, as extended sRGB values, lands on
   the target.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/extreme-mastering: ACES AP0 (a negative y) and a
   gamut at the protocol's coordinate limit, which the mastering
   acceptance rule takes, are still accepted as mastering primaries;
   the op is present exactly when the predicate holds, and the
   conversion stays finite.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/threshold-unchanged: samples within the threshold
   (including d = 0.8 exactly), without a positive achromatic value,
   and, for a source whose red primary alone exceeds the target, the
   red and blue channels that have no limit above 1, come out of the op
   bit for bit.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/reference: the op matches the independent formula,
   including far outside the gamut and for dark samples, and maps a
   distance equal to a channel's limit onto the boundary.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/monotonic: in each channel, compressed distances
   increase with the input distance from 0 to 4, never exceed it, and
   equal it bit for bit up to the threshold.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/non-finite: samples with an infinite or not
   positive achromatic value come out bit for bit; huge and infinitely
   negative channels give no NaN.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/source-gamut: sRGB encoded content mastered on
   BT.2020 is not compressed, on a declared sRGB or a wider target;
   BT.2020 content mastered on P3, on a declared sRGB target that
   excludes part of P3, is compressed with the limits of P3, matching
   the formula with them and not with BT.2020's.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/tolerance: on a target declaring P3 through 12-bit
   DisplayID coordinates, with one step as its tolerance, P3 primaries
   lie a fraction of a step outside the quantized triangle and are not
   compressed (an exact test would compress them), while a P3 red moved
   five steps outside one side is; boundary points within the tolerance
   set no limit, so the blue channel, beyond 1 only within the
   tolerance, is never rewritten, and the op matches the formula with
   the limits of the points outside.
 - /color-hdr/gamut/shader-matches-cpu: the GLSL compression matches
   the CPU one through the gamma 2.2 and forced-linear blending states
   and together with highlight mapping, with exact floating point input.

And a compositor regression, /wayland/mapping/target-gamut, run in its
own invocation since it gives the fixture output declared primaries
(DCI-P3 with a D65 white, rounded to DisplayID 2 12-bit CIE 1931
coordinates, with no target peak): the view's blending state carries
exactly the published target primaries, with the tolerances the device
derived for the declaration, and no peak; a BT.2020 PQ surface, in the
output's encoding, is compressed, refused scanout by
meta_compositor_view_native_color_state_prevents_scanout() and rendered
differently from the untargeted conversion; the same encoding mastered
on real P3 primaries (0.680, 0.320 / 0.265, 0.690 / 0.150, 0.060) lies
within the declaration's tolerance, so it is neither compressed nor
refused scanout, while P3 with its red primary 1% further from white
still compresses; content mastered with sRGB primaries is neither
compressed nor refused scanout; an sRGB surface is never compressed;
and once the primaries fall back to the encoding's, nothing is
compressed and BT.2020 PQ is scanned out again.

The earlier target-primaries fixture is not reused for it: its blue
primary leaves sRGB's blue outside the triangle, so sRGB content would
be compressed there.

And /native-hdr/target-primaries-tolerance: declared target primaries
carry one 12-bit step as each primary's tolerance for CIE 1931
coordinates and none for the encoding fallback; for CIE 1976
coordinates each primary's tolerance, which differs between primaries,
is at least sqrt (2) times the exact xy displacement half a code step in
any direction causes at that primary.

